\paragraph{Reward saturation.} When all $K$ rollouts receive the same binary reward, $\sigma = 0$ and $\hat{A}_k = 0$, producing zero gradient. At $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$, the all-fail probability is $(1{-}p)^K \approx 34.4\%$; combined with the negligible all-success case ($p^K < 10^{-7}$), roughly one-third of batches yield no gradient at all. This compounds sign misattribution: of the ${\sim}66\%$ of batches that produce a gradient, only 19.1\% have the correct sign. The effective learning rate is $0.66 \times 0.19 \approx 12.6\%$ of nominal---well below the regime where convergence guarantees hold.

\paragraph{Cumulative divergence.} Sign misattribution produces a systematic gradient bias. Let $\theta^*$ denote an optimal policy parameterization. At each update, the expected gradient under step-independent training is:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}] = P_c \cdot g_+ + (1 - P_c) \cdot g_-
\label{eq:biased_gradient}
\end{equation}
where $P_c = P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update})$, $g_+$ points toward $\theta^*$, and $g_-$ points away. When $P_c < 0.5$ (which holds for $p < 0.5$; Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}), the expected gradient points \textit{away} from good policies. After $t$ steps: $\|\theta_t - \theta^*\| \geq \|\theta_0 - \theta^*\| + t \cdot \eta (1 - 2P_c) \|g\|$, where $\eta$ is the learning rate. This is not slow convergence but monotonic divergence---each step increases the distance to good policies, explaining the two-phase collapse observed empirically.

